    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the technical side, but i'm worried about the 'objective behavioral triggers' part in practice. if the model sees a high density of certain demographic groups in an area with historically high crime stats, even if we use SHAP to show it's 'behavioral,' isn't it almost inevitable that the model will end up flagging those specific communities more often?\n\nlike, if we're trying to be 'real' about urban management, isn't it true that certain racial or ethnic makeup in high-density areas often correlates with that 'socioeconomic instability' we talked about? how do we stop the model from just confirming existing stereotypes—basically saying \"this area is high risk because of who lives there\"—even if we try to mask it with fancy math?",
